"A consistent hash ring maps keys and node tokens into an ordered hash space to explain partition ownership and reassignment.\n\nUse when: Explain key placement and node membership changes. Review virtual-node or replica assignment rules.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is physical network wiring or a chronological cycle.\n\nVisual grammar: Angular position represents hash-space order rather than geography or time. Token markers identify owning nodes or virtual nodes. Key markers and directed arcs show lookup and replica selection under stated rules.\n\nBuild: Declare hash-space ordering and ownership convention. Place a small set of node tokens and synthetic key hashes. Assign ranges and show the effect of one membership change. Keep token labels outside the ring with short noncrossing leader lines.\n\nCheck: Wraparound ownership is handled correctly. Replica placement follows the named algorithm and failure-domain rules. Equal visual arcs are not presented as measured equal load. Leader lines and range labels do not cross token names.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming uniform hash ranges imply uniform traffic or data size. Confusing a logical token ring with network topology.\n\nExample: A new token takes responsibility for one hash interval, while the remaining intervals retain their owners.\n\nAlternatives: Storage partition diagram, Replication topology."
